1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Welcome to Multi AI agent systems
with CrewAI,

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.833
built in partnership with CrewAI and taught by
its founder and CEO, João Moura.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.900
I think AI agent workflows will be a key
driver of AI progress in the near term.

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.333
Multi agent workflows
let you break down the complex

5
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.700
tasks into subtasks
that can be executed by agents,

6
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.700
each of which plays
a particular defined role.

7
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.633
For example,
if your goal is get a research report

8
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:31.233
written, the roles might be researcher,
writer, and fact checker.

9
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:34.300
Or if you want to get a website built,
perhaps

10
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.033
the roles could be a web designer,
software engineer and testing engineer.

11
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.533
I've really enjoyed using crewAI myself,
and I'm delighted that its creator

12
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.533
João, is teaching this course.

13
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:49.333
João had created crewAI when he needed a tool
to build better agents,

14
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:52.800
to help him write LinkedIn posts,
and he has a lot of experience

15
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.033
designing workflows
to collections of agents.

16
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:56.700
Execute.

17
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.600
Thanks, Andrew.

18
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:03.300
Really, I'm excited to work with
you and your team and this is and

19
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.000
has the potential to enable engineers
to build great things.

20
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.100
In this course,
you learn the major building blocks

21
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.233
for agentic systems
with an emphasis on multi-agent systems.

22
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.933
Building blocks
you learn include: role-playing, tool use,

23
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.700
memory, guardrails, and collaboration.

24
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.400
And you use these components
to build a collection of agents,

25
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.600
to customize a resume, to job description,
and to perform financial analysis,

26
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.300
and also to perform event planning. As part
of assembling your agentic workflow,

27
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:39.800
you also define
how these agents cooperate,

28
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:43.333
which ones are able to delegate
to other agents to perform

29
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:47.233
certain tasks like research,
and whether certain agents should execute

30
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:51.600
their tasks in parallel or in series,
or in a hierarchical fashion,

31
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.100
with a manager agent
delegating to a number of worker agents.

32
00:01:55.433 --> 00:01:59.400
João will go through these concepts
using the crewAI Open Source library.

33
00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:03.900
Yes, this course will help engineers
trying to understand

34
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:08.200
how to build AI agentic applications
and how they differ

35
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.200
from regular applications that
we have been building up to this point.

36
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:16.700
After watching this,
you will be equipped with everything

37
00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:21.633
you need to build multi-agent systems
and reap all the benefits from it.

38
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.200
I like to think of good multi-agent
systems designs like being a manager.

39
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.100
You are now promoted
into this manager of agents

40
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.033
and get to identify the goals
and the various roles

41
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:38.133
that will work together
to achieve those goals,

42
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.633
and also define clear expectations
for what success looks like.

43
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.533
It's a very interesting mental shift
from regular engineering.

44
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:49.100
Yes, indeed,

45
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.800
many people
have worked to create this course.

46
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.433
I'd like to thank the whole CrewAI
team and from DeepLearning.AI,

47
00:02:56.433 --> 00:02:59.033
Eddy Shyu,
also contributed to this course.

48
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.300
The first lesson will give you
an overview of AI agents,

49
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.400
and also an overview
of the rest of the course.

50
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.733
So make sure to stick around.

51
00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:12.000
So let's go on to the next video
and dive into the key

52
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.000
building blocks of AI agents.

